Fix RET_SUBMIT desynchronising the Linux vhci_hcd client.

Applies on top of viiper-v0.7.0-asb.patch.

A USB/IP RET_SUBMIT carries a data payload only for IN transfers. The server
was appending the handler's response buffer to OUT replies as well. usbip-win2,
which VIIPER is developed against, tolerates the extra bytes; the Linux kernel
client does not. It reads them as the beginning of the next PDU, finds a
command field of zero, logs "vhci_hcd: unknown pdu 0" and tears the device
down a few seconds into enumeration.

Observed against a DualSense with the ApexSenseBridge composite descriptor: the
device enumerated, strings and configuration descriptors were read correctly,
and the teardown began at the first audio-class SET_CUR, which is the first
OUT control transfer the kernel issues.

--- a/internal/server/usb/server.go
+++ b/internal/server/usb/server.go
@@
 		if isIsoTransfer && dir == usbip.DirOut && len(respIsoPackets) > 0 {
 			scheduleIsoOutSubmit(seq, ret, respIsoPackets)
-		} else if err := writeRetSubmit(ret, respData, respIsoPackets); err != nil {
+		} else if err := writeRetSubmit(ret, respPayload, respIsoPackets); err != nil {
 			return err
 		}
@@
+		// RET_SUBMIT carries a data payload only for IN transfers. Appending
+		// one to an OUT reply desynchronises the stream: the Linux vhci_hcd
+		// client reads those bytes as the start of the next PDU, reports
+		// "unknown pdu 0" and tears the device down.
+		respPayload := respData
+		if dir == usbip.DirOut {
+			respPayload = nil
+		}
